cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code, please cite the paper."
title: "Tokens-per-Parameter Coverage Is Critical for Robust LLM Scaling Law Extrapolation"
type: software
license: GPL-2.0-only
version: "neurips2026-camera-ready"
date-released: "2026-11-09"
abstract: >-
  Neural scaling laws approximate a language model's loss as a power-law function of parameter count
  $N$ and token count $D$. Following Chinchilla-style compute-optimal training, many studies fit
  scaling laws from runs at one or a few fixed tokens-per-parameter (TPP) ratios $k$, with $D = kN$.
  We show that this collinear design, combined with the empirically common near-equality of the
  exponents governing $N$ and $D$, induces an inherent ill-conditioning in the Gauss-Newton
  least-squares problem: the condition number of the design grows as the inverse square of the gap
  between the $N$- and $D$-exponents. The scale coefficients become practically unidentifiable, with
  confidence intervals inflating by orders of magnitude, yielding a "sloppy" model whose
  extrapolations degrade sharply off the training ray. We prove this for four scaling-law formalisms
  and derive a closed-form TPP-diversity threshold that characterizes well-conditioned estimation to
  leading order. Empirically, in controlled experiments with 5M-77M-parameter models, non-collinear
  designs outperform collinear ones on held-out data with a 97.3% win rate across four laws and five
  corpora, and 98.7% in bfloat16. We further show the degeneracy is rooted in Jacobian geometry and
  is not an artifact of the loss function: any smooth objective whose Gauss-Newton weights are
  bounded above and away from zero inherits the same ill-conditioning.
